# GUI Appearance

## Design principles

1. **Peer composition before tokens** — match an existing page model first;
   `fw-*` classes alone do not fix a wrong layout.
2. **One job per section** — one purpose, one clear primary action when needed.
3. **Chrome earns its border** — structure stays flat and transparent; borders
   delimit data regions or adjacent panes, not decoration.
4. **Interactive things look interactive** — controls, hovers, overlays, and
   flashes carry fill; panels and headers do not.
5. **Headers only when they carry title or actions** — no decorative subtitles
   or repeated page titles in widget chrome.
6. **Dense operator UI, not marketing** — tool density and chrome economy win
   over heroes, gradients, and brand-landing composition.
7. **Live eye-check beats “classes look right”** — fail “technically works but
   looks bad” at the four review viewports.

## Visual language (develop to this)

Operator GUI is a **flat, full-bleed, dense tool surface** — not a marketing
site and not a card dashboard.

| Pillar | Spec |
| --- | --- |
| Flat / solid | Solid `--fw-*` fills only. No CSS/Tailwind gradients. Soft feathers use `color-mix` / opacity overlays into `--fw-app-bg` or `--fw-page-bg`, never `bg-gradient-*`. |
| Full-bleed shell | Nav, page header, page footer, and panels abut with hairline rules. `--fw-panel-gap` stays `0`. No viewport-centered nav inset at any band (including 4K). |
| Transparent structure | Panels/headers/tables sit on app background; fill is for controls, hover/active, pills, flashes, overlays. |
| Square chrome | Structural surfaces are square. Interactive controls use `--fw-radius-md` squares — **no** pill/circle buttons. Round geometry is only for non-interactive marks (dots, avatars, meters). |
| Pale semantics | Info/success/warning/danger via `--fw-semantic-*` pale tints. Primary UI pair is `--fw-nav-active-bg` / `--fw-nav-active-text` (not chart blue). |
| Type | `font-normal` data, `font-medium` labels/controls, `font-semibold` titles; never `font-bold`/`font-black`. Floor `text-xs`. |
| Density | Operator density: icon-only secondary actions, constant page-header height, stable action-bar geometry across peer routes. |
| Tokens | Only `--fw-*` / `fw-*` from `framework/runtimes/gui/src/main.css`. Invented hex, undefined aliases, or sibling-repo palettes fail review. |

When in doubt: open a system peer under `framework/addons/system/gui/` and copy
**composition**, then verify live at the four viewports.

## Scope

These rules own **framework and addon operator GUI** (pages, widgets, sections,
settings, tables, dialogs) rendered by the GUI runtime.

They do **not** own public marketing / landing / promotional surfaces. Do not
“improve” operator pages toward hero layouts, full-bleed marketing imagery,
expressive display fonts, gradient atmospheres, or card-heavy promotional
sections. If a surface is truly marketing-only, keep it out of this contract
rather than bending these rules.

In-repo source of truth for tokens and geometry is
`framework/runtimes/gui/src/main.css` — not external design systems or sibling
repos.

## Research gate (new addon / large GUI)

Before writing or heavily expanding GUI for a **new addon** or a **major feature
with many new GUI elements** (new pages, new `component_type` renderers,
or a new control surface), do a short research pass — even when a thin stub
already exists. Do not invent a layout from AC text alone.

1. **Read the docs / rules that own the surface** — this rule,
   `framework-gui-page-patterns.mdc`, `framework-addon-renderers.mdc`,
   `framework-icon-buttons.mdc` when actions are involved, and the relevant
   hand-authored Sphinx pages (`docs/sphinx/source/gui-page-patterns.md`,
   `docs/sphinx/source/first-addon.md` for new addons).
2. **Pick an archetype** — use the table in `framework-gui-page-patterns.mdc`
   (or the Sphinx page) and open one or two peers that solve the same job.
3. **Find peer references** — prefer system primitives under
   `framework/addons/system/gui/` and nearby framework addons that already ship
   the same kind of page. Copy their **composition**
   (`SectionFrame`, `WidgetSectionHeader`, `FormSection` / labeled
   `fw-input fw-surface` fields, `fw-table` / `fw-list-section`,
   `SectionEmptyState`, `fw-button fw-surface fw-surface-thin`), not only their
   colors.
4. **Prefer base components** — reuse framework renderers and shared controls.
   Add a custom renderer or addon-local chrome only when an existing primitive
   cannot meet a real requirement; if you must diverge, document the gap next
   to the custom selector (nearest primitive considered + why it fails).

Skip this gate for trivial edits (typo, one-line fix, token tweak on an
existing control). Appearance tokens alone do not fix a wrong layout model —
thematic unity comes from peer composition first.

Settings live where their base class dictates (`BaseSystemSetting` → system
settings surfaces; `BaseUserSetting` → user settings). Deep links and nav
targets must match that placement — do not park a user setting under system
settings (or the reverse) because an AC named a category string.

## Interaction and state

- **Action hierarchy** — at most one primary text-labeled action per
  **page chrome**, **section header**, or **open modal** (Create / Save /
  Sign in). A page may keep its primary while a nested dialog has its own
  (page Create + dialog Save is fine). Compact, secondary, repeated, toolbar,
  row, and header actions are icon-only per `framework-icon-buttons.mdc`.
- **Busy** — show pending on the busy control within one frame (`aria-busy`,
  disabled, spinner). Do not default to a page-wide busy overlay
  (`framework-gui-live-updates.mdc`).
- **Empty** — use `SectionEmptyState` with short factual copy inside the
  section; do not invent empty-state illustration chrome.
- **Loading** — first cold load may use skeleton / Loading panel; deferred
  sections stay section-local on failure (`deferred_section`).
- **Error / danger** — pale semantic roles (`--fw-semantic-*`); header deletes
  use `.fw-action-badge-danger`; confirm-dialog and inline form errors use
  `.fw-danger-text`. Prefer shared `ConfirmDialog` for destructive confirms.
- **Focus and hit targets** — keep keyboard focus visible; icon and text
  controls stay on `--fw-icon-control-size` / `--fw-control-height`. Icon-only
  controls need `aria-label` + `title` (icon-buttons rule).
- **Motion** — prefer subtle opacity or transform only; honor
  `prefers-reduced-motion`. No decorative motion, gradient animation, or
  attention-seeking loops on operator chrome.

## Visual done rubric

After appearance changes, click through the live GUI at the four review
viewports (`mobile` 390×844, `tablet` 768×1024, `desktop` 1920×1080, `4k`
3840×2160 — see `docs/sphinx/source/gui-visual-harness.md` and
`framework/runtimes/gui/src/viewports.js`). Fail the handoff if any check fails.
Do not require automated screenshots or a capture script.

- Can the reader find the primary action in under two seconds?
- Does the layout match a peer archetype (not a one-off panel stack)?
- Any doubled seam, floating gutter, boxed checklist, or invented chrome?
- Do empty, loading, and error states look intentional and section-local?
- Does the narrow viewport keep nav usable without crushing Menu or actions?
- At 4K, does chrome use width elegantly without stretching prose full-bleed?

Engineering invariants alone are not enough. Buttons, modals, spacing,
alignment, color restraint, chrome economy, and readability must pass live
browser inspection. "Technically works but looks bad" is a fail. Geometric
helpers in `framework/runtimes/gui/tests/visual/helpers.js`
(`assertNoRoundButtons`, `assertNoDoubledSeams`) catch invariants that eye
review still misses.

Structure and renderer contracts remain in `framework-react.mdc` and
`framework-addon-renderers.mdc`. Page models live in
`framework-gui-page-patterns.mdc`.

## Tokens, geometry, and chrome contracts

- Reuse `fw-*` semantic classes and `--fw-*` tokens from `framework/runtimes/gui/src/main.css`. No new hex colors, `shadow-black`/ad-hoc shadows, or palette Tailwind color classes when a token exists.
- Soft semantic roles (info / success / warning / danger) are theme-independent: `--fw-semantic-*` in `main.css` is the single pale palette for flashes, action badges, danger text, status rings, and related accents. Themes only mix those accents into their surface for flash fills — never reintroduce hard saturated hex fills for those roles (including `debug`). Prefer pale tinted action badges over solid hard red/green/amber plates. Header danger actions use `.fw-action-badge-danger` so they match list/table delete chips; keep `.fw-danger-text` for confirm-dialog buttons and inline form errors, not for header icon deletes.
- Themes are registerable ``BaseTheme`` components (framework ``themes`` addon
  ships ``dark``, ``light``, ``debug``). Selector options come from the registry,
  not a hardcoded tuple. Builtin token CSS stays in `main.css`; other addons
  contribute ``[data-theme="…"]`` blocks from their GUI entry and optional
  ``themes`` metadata. First paint uses an inline ``index.html`` bootstrap from
  ``framework.theme`` / ``framework.theme.colorScheme`` so cold load does not
  flash the wrong scheme. `dark` and `light` follow the in-repo token scheme in
  `framework/runtimes/gui/src/main.css`; `debug` is owned here.
- Density and chrome follow the in-repo shadcn-scale tokens in `main.css`, the same source of truth as the colors. Radii are `--fw-radius-sm/md/lg` = `0.375/0.5/0.625rem`; controls are `--fw-control-height` (`h-9`) and `--fw-icon-control-size` (`size-8`); shadows never exceed `--fw-shadow` (`shadow-xs`) on chrome or `--fw-shadow-raised` (`shadow-md`) on overlays. Do not reintroduce heavy diffuse shadows. Every button is a square with `--fw-radius-md` corners, including icon-only controls, toggles, and overlay action badges — there are no circle or pill buttons anywhere in the app, and a control that needs its own radius rule is drift. A fully round radius (`999px`, `rounded-full`, `50%`) belongs only to non-interactive marks: dots, swatches, meters, avatars, and status pills. `assertNoRoundButtons` in `framework/runtimes/gui/tests/visual/helpers.js` enforces this geometrically, since a radius at or past half the short side is what makes a control read as round.
- Layout is flat and full-bleed: structural panels abut and divide with a single hairline rule, never with a gutter. `--fw-panel-gap` is `0` and stays `0` — do not reintroduce spacing between panels via `gap`, `margin`, or page padding. The page has no outer padding; nav, page header, and page footer are full-bleed rules (`border-bottom`/`border-top`, no radius, no fill); widget chrome carries no radius and no shadow, taking a bottom rule when stacked and a right rule when side by side. `.fw-surface` is square by default and controls opt back in: radius belongs to buttons, button groups, inputs, badges, nav links, list rows, and overlays only. Panels, headers of every level (page, section, widget), tables, form groups, cards, and list areas are square — do not restore a radius on a structural class. The blanket `border-width: 0` chrome reset in `main.css` is unlayered and later in source order, so any selector listed there loses its own divider no matter how the rule above it reads; a structural class that must keep a rule (page header, page footer) is deliberately absent from that list rather than fighting it with a longer selector or `!important`.
- A seam has exactly one owner, because two 1px rules meeting at the same y render as one 2px line and read as a mistake. The preceding block's `border-bottom` owns a stacked seam, so a following block never adds `border-top` to the same edge; a full-bleed panel (`fw-panel`, addon shells) draws no outer box at all, since the page header above and widget chrome below already own those edges. Divider rules written for stacked children must exclude column layouts (`.fw-widget-body:not(.fw-widget-body-section-columns) > .fw-section-frame:not(:last-child)`) — in a column the previous sibling is to the left, so a bottom rule is not a divider and just doubles the widget's own bottom edge. Columns divide with `border-left`. In a wrapping grid of abutting boxes (`fw-card-collection`) no `:not(:last-child)` rule can work, since a selector cannot tell a row end from the grid edge: each card paints only its bottom and right, and the container supplies the outer top and left. Collapse rules must name every class that actually lands in the container — `fw-card-nested` does not carry `fw-card`, so a rule written only for `.fw-card` silently skips nested stacks. A box that only sometimes abuts a rule keys off the first-child chain rather than dropping its edge outright: `.fw-table` keeps its top border in general, and gives it up only as `.fw-widget-body > .fw-section-frame:first-child > .fw-section-content:first-child > .fw-table:first-child`, since a table under a section header or in a later frame has nothing above it and would otherwise lose a real edge. `assertNoDoubledSeams` in `tests/visual/helpers.js` enforces this geometrically across layout archetypes; pixel diffing does not catch it.
- Padding belongs to leaf content, not to structural containers. Interior spacing uses `--fw-content-pad` / `--fw-content-pad-tight`; `--fw-layout-gap` and `--fw-layout-rim-gap` are interior values that addons consume, not panel gaps. A widget body pads its content by default, and drops that padding when its child is a data region (`fw-list-section`, `fw-table-section`, `fw-kanban-section`) or an addon `fw-panel`, which own their edges and run to the widget border. Mark full-bleed addon panels with `fw-panel` rather than zeroing padding ad hoc. The same exemption applies to `section_columns` panes: a data region gets no pane padding, because a pane inset stacks with the data area's own and leaves rows floating in a double gutter. When a data region drops its pane padding, its section header must carry its own inset (`--fw-content-pad-tight 1rem`) so the title lines up with where the first row's content begins instead of sitting flush to the edge.
- Compact list rails use a compact list-rail pattern: 8px gap between rows, `py-2 px-3 text-sm` rows on an 8px radius, 8px/4px data-area padding, no row border, `--fw-button-hover-bg` fill on hover and active, `font-normal` idle and `font-medium` active. The active marker is a 2px `--fw-nav-active-bg` pill inset 6px from the row's top and bottom, never a flush full-height rule — a flush bar is clipped by the row's rounding and reads as a stray border.
- `--fw-nav-active-bg` / `--fw-nav-active-text` are the UI primary pair: dark is white on dark text `oklch(0.985 0 0)`/`oklch(0.205 0 0)`, light inverts it, `debug` is `#db2777`/`#ffffff`. Both are per-theme — never define the foreground once in the base block, and never consume the fill without its paired text color (that is how an active pill turns white-on-white). Data visualization uses `--fw-chart-series-0` (chart slot 0), not the primary; the saturated blue `oklch(0.488 0.243 264.376)` belongs to that chart slot, not the nav.
- Every theme's `--fw-app-bg` / `--fw-page-bg` must be the surface its text was written for, since panels are transparent and the app background is what the reader actually sees. Docs chrome (`docs/sphinx/source/_static/custom.css`, `conf.py`) tracks the `dark` ramp and moves with it.
- Panels have no fill; the border does the work. `--fw-widget-chrome-bg`, `--fw-widget-body-bg`, `--fw-card-bg`, `--fw-table-bg`, and `--fw-table-header-bg` are `transparent` on `dark` and `light`, and stay defined only so addon stylesheets resolve and `debug` can light each layer up to expose nesting. Nav, page/section/widget headers, widget body, footer, sidebar, cards, grid items, kanban lanes, and table header cells all sit on the app background. Fill is reserved for things that must read as interactive or floating: controls (`--fw-control-bg`, inputs), hover/active states (`--fw-button-hover-bg`, `--fw-nav-active-bg`), pills, flashes, and overlays. Modals are the concrete case, and the one place the transparent-panel default is wrong: a dialog dims the page and must read as floating above it, so a dialog painted in the app background is invisible against the page behind it no matter how correct its computed style looks. The panel (`fw-modal-panel`, `--fw-modal-bg`) sits one step lighter than `--fw-app-bg`; its bars (`fw-modal-header` / `fw-modal-footer`, `--fw-modal-header-bg`) fall back to the app background so the header reads as a distinct bar; the scrim is `fw-dialog-overlay` at one shared opacity. Never a page-local background, never an addon rule that re-declares `--fw-card-bg` or its own scrim over them. Verify a dialog by sampling rendered pixels, not `getComputedStyle` — a header inheriting the panel's fill computes as "set" while being the same color as everything around it.
- Chrome economy is structural, not decorative: section surfaces, page body/row/column, and form items are transparent, and form fields sit bare on the pane instead of inside a boxed group. A border must earn its place by delimiting a data widget (table, card, grid item) or dividing adjacent panes.
- Collection tiles must reserve geometry before media loads (`aspectRatio` on the thumbnail shell; `containIntrinsicSize` when using `content-visibility`) so lazy/windowed grids do not CLS when images arrive.
- Type scale: `font-normal` for data, `font-medium` for labels/controls/nav, `font-semibold` for titles; never `font-bold`/`font-black`. The size floor is `text-xs` (12px) — no bespoke `text-[0.65rem]`/`text-[11px]`. `uppercase` is reserved for table header cells and always without `tracking-*`.
- `main.css` rules are unlayered and beat every Tailwind utility. Never redeclare a preflight `@layer base` reset (`button,input,select,textarea{font:inherit}`) unlayered — it silently kills `text-*`/`font-*` on all controls. Control chrome carries its own padding/type utilities (`fw-button fw-surface fw-surface-thin px-3 py-1.5 text-xs`), not per-surface CSS patches.
- `.fw-surface` must set an explicit `--fw-*` `border-color` (default `--fw-widget-chrome-border`); never `currentColor` (it resolves near-white `--fw-text` on dark themes). `fw-surface-thin` only narrows the width, so it is never worn alone: Tailwind preflight resets every element to `border: 0 solid`, and a thin surface without `fw-surface` restores a width against the initial `currentColor` — a harsh near-white rule on the dark themes. `guiPrimitiveComposition.test.js` gates the pair. Prefer semantic section classes (`fw-list-section`, `fw-form-section`, list rows) over bare `fw-surface` for settings chrome.
- Repeated selectable rows — checkbox lists, option pickers, filter menus — are `fw-list-row` (`px-3 py-2 text-sm`, `--fw-radius-md`, transparent until the `--fw-button-hover-bg` hover fill), not per-row borders. A checklist that boxes each item is drift: rows in a bordered scroll area are already delimited by the container's `fw-input fw-surface` edge, and a rule per row turns a list into a stack of cards.
- Flat only: no CSS/Tailwind gradients (`linear/radial/conic-gradient`, `bg-gradient-*`/`from-*`/`via-*`/`to-*`) on any theme or surface. Use solid `--fw-*` fills; meters use solid fills, SVG strokes, or width bars — not gradient rings. Backdrop / hero art that should “fade into the page” uses a solid `color-mix` (or opacity) overlay toward `--fw-app-bg` / `--fw-page-bg` — that is still flat-only, not a gradient utility.
- Native `<select>` option popups follow the active theme: `select option` /
  `select optgroup` use `--fw-control-bg` and `--fw-text` in `main.css`. Do
  **not** force `color-scheme: light` or Canvas/CanvasText on option lists —
  that paints white OS dropdowns on dark themes site-wide.
- Floating / sticky notices (consent, cookie, toasts, sticky banners) must use
  real `--fw-*` fills (`--fw-control-bg`, `--fw-modal-bg`, or a documented
  flash token) — never undefined aliases like a bare `--fw-surface` /
  `--fw-ink`. Stack them at the top of the overlay ladder (at or above flash /
  dialog z-index in `main.css`, currently flash `2000`) so new chrome cannot
  cover them. Verify by sampling rendered pixels, not only computed style.
- High-resolution chrome: above full-HD (`min-width: 120.01rem`), `main.css`
  scales type, `--fw-control-height`, `--fw-icon-control-size`, content pads,
  and `--fw-chrome-scale` by **1.5**. Compose shared `fw-button` / `fw-input` /
  `fw-nav-*` / `fw-tag` / `fw-badge` so hit targets scale with text. Do **not**
  enlarge root font-size, browser zoom, or Tailwind `--spacing` for 4K (that
  also blows up media grids). Page chrome may span `--fw-page-max-width`;
  prose / empty-state / footer copy stay on `--fw-readable-max-width`.
- Brand mark: SVG (or masked) logos tint with `--fw-nav-brand-mark`. Crop
  uploaded SVG viewBoxes tight to ink — padded canvases make the mark look
  tiny inside the fixed rem box (~`1.75rem`). Avoid a cold-load flash of the
  default mark before the custom logo resolves (bootstrap / branding fetch
  must not paint the fallback then swap).
- Media shells: reserve geometry (`aspectRatio`, `containIntrinsicSize`) before
  paint. Grid / card tiles default to **cover** fill of that shell
  (`object-cover` via `Thumbnail`); preview / lightbox defaults to **contain**.
  Do not double-letterbox (outer padded box + inner contain). Ephemeral player
  chrome (LIVE badges, sparse HUD) prefers hover/focus reveal over permanent
  clutter on the media plane.
- Auth login composition: local username/password (and primary Sign in) first;
  OAuth / external IdP buttons **below** a clear separator; each OAuth control
  keeps `fw-button fw-surface fw-surface-thin` with a leading brand glyph when
  a widely understood icon exists. Operator density — not a marketing hero.
- Peer-route chrome stability: page header height and trailing action clusters
  must not jump when switching sibling routes that share a shell (Home ↔
  section, list ↔ detail). Prefer icon-only secondaries so text labels cannot
  shift neighbor icons; keep a documented action order per surface.
- Failed / denied pages are centered muted empty states (nav already owns the
  top seam) — not a full-viewport danger-bordered box. Soft-nav revalidation
  dims in place (`.fw-app-shell--revalidating`); do not invent a second
  full-page busy takeover.
- One overflow owner: page/widget chrome must not spill its frame. Tables, lists, and long content scroll inside `SectionFrame` or the section content region, not outside the bordered surface. Tables with long identifiers/paths use fixed table layout with cell widths and truncation/break so actions stay in the scrollport.
- Row actions live in one trailing column built by `actions_column(count=…)` (`panel_form_helpers.py`) — never a hand-rolled `{"key": "actions", …}` literal, which is how the old `Actions`/blank header split and the 8%/10%/18%/8rem width spread crept in. `test_table_actions_column.py` fails on a literal. The column carries no visible header text (the renderers supply the `Actions` accessible name), `TableSection`/`TableInput` right-align it, and its width is a `min-width` floor: a fixed-layout table hands the trailing non-percentage column all leftover space, so a `max-width` there would clip the buttons. A pane that hand-rolls its own `<table>` imports `actionsColumnStyle` / `ACTIONS_CELL_CONTENT_CLASS_NAME` from `table/actionsColumn.js` instead of restyling the cell.
- Text controls use `--fw-control-height`; icon-only and section-collapse controls use `--fw-icon-control-size` and must not inherit the taller text-control min-height.
- Preserve flex fill: keep `min-h-0`/`min-w-0` through page→row→column→widget→section; compose producer width roles with them and don't replace `min-w-0` when adding a min-width floor. Detail panes that swap nested `FormSection`/`SectionFrame` use `display:contents` when a real `flex-col` wrapper would make `flex-1` grow height while hug/`self-start` leaves a horizontal void. Hug frames with `flex-1` in `width_class_name` stretch (`self-stretch`+`w-full`); fixed sidebars (`w-72`) keep `self-start`.
- Sections hug by default (`fill_height=False`). Opt into `fill_height=True` only for intentional fill scrollports (Jobs status tables; card/table/grid/kanban demo peers and panel main peers); keep short admin lists and hug list demos on hug. Do not flip the global default to fix demo voids.
- Use named width roles (`sidebar`, `main`, `collapsed-rail`) over ad-hoc Tailwind width strings on new producers. Reuse `SectionFrame`, `WidgetSectionHeader`, shared buttons/surfaces, and modal/dialog primitives instead of page-local chrome. New features and ports stay framework-driven; bespoke UI is forbidden unless a significant primitive limitation is documented at the custom component/selector with the unsatisfied requirement and nearest primitive considered.
- Side-collapse rails stretch to the `section_columns` row height. Do not content-hug collapsed left/right rails (`align-self:flex-start` or `self-start` on the frame); keep icon controls at `--fw-icon-control-size`.
- Custom addon CSS consumes `--fw-*` tokens. New buttons use `fw-button fw-surface fw-surface-thin` (or a documented addon class wrapping the same tokens).
- Primitive composition is mandatory, not cosmetic: addon controls use the same complete class composition as their framework owners — editable fields `fw-input fw-surface` + framework-standard padding; badges/tags `fw-badge`/`fw-tag` with `fw-surface fw-surface-thin`; buttons per the shared button composition. Bare semantic color classes, bespoke textareas, badge-like colored spans, and addon-local copies of framework controls fail review. A composite whose parent intentionally owns border/radius may omit a class only with an adjacent comment starting `primitive-composite:` (so the static gate can verify it); repeated such controls may also carry `data-primitive-composite="<reason>"` at each usage for source and rendered-DOM audits. `tests/guiPrimitiveComposition.test.js` gates this across every addon from one repo-wide test, so a filtered Vitest run scoped to your own component files reports success while your controls violate it — finish GUI work with the full `npm run test` in `framework/runtimes/gui`.
- Icon-only controls, their glyph vocabulary, and their class/accessibility composition are owned by `framework-icon-buttons.mdc`; that rule governs every compact, repeated, toolbar, row, and header action here.
- At `max-width:48rem` (`--fw-bp-md`), keep the top nav a single nowrap row: hide `.fw-nav-primary`, show `.fw-nav-primary-mobile` (Menu), hide the brand wordmark (mark only), and collapse Menu / More / account triggers to icon-only so header actions never crush the Menu to width 0. Do not reintroduce wrap on `.fw-nav` for narrow viewports. Keep `.fw-nav` edge-to-edge at every band (including above `--fw-bp-xl`): brand and actions use the base nav padding only — no viewport-centered inset. Shell bootstrap mirrors the active band onto `<html data-fw-bp>`.
- The top nav is a full-bleed header bar. The brand is a borderless lockup (`NavBrand`): the Framework mark tinted with `--fw-nav-brand-mark`, then the app-name wordmark behind a left divider tinted `color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 25%, transparent)` — deliberately not `--fw-nav-control-border` (too dark on this background). Do not box the brand or reintroduce a `--fw-nav-brand-border` surface. Primary items sit in one bordered, rounded `.fw-nav-primary` group of ghost pills (a button-group exception to the square-container rule; keep the border and radius). Header dropdown triggers (Menu, More, account) use `.fw-nav-control` (bordered, filled), not `.fw-nav-link`. Mobile Menu open state is session-only (`useState`); never persist it through `useUiStorage`.
- Header economy: emit headers only when essential. Keep the page header title; page headers carry no subtitle at all — `BasePageHeader` has no such field, so qualifying detail belongs in the body or a widget/section header. The page header and footer keep a constant height across routes via `min-height: calc(var(--fw-control-height) + 2 * var(--fw-content-pad-tight))`, so a page with no actions renders the same bar as one with buttons; do not drop that floor or reintroduce content that makes the bar's height route-dependent. Keep a widget/section header only when it hosts actions or a title that distinguishes siblings. Do not repeat the page title in a widget header (hide that bar when it has no actions) or emit decorative/obvious subtitles (auto-save reminders, "setting groups", filler "Showing {category} settings"). Do not enable left-panel collapse on production settings or navigation pages, including Jobs; keep shared left-collapse for purpose-built dense surfaces and the system example/sandbox collapse demos.
